Jahshaka patch 0059 — a v2 Mesh built in memory can be given its LOD values.

SOURCE patch. EVERY TREE MUST RE-RUN `irisgl/scripts/build-ogre.sh` after
pulling this. It touches `OgreMain/include/OgreMesh2.h` and
`OgreMain/src/OgreMesh2.cpp`, which NO other patch in this stack touches, so
there is no overlap to reset for.

WHY A PATCH AND NOT A WORKAROUND (owner rule, 2026-09-15, ledger §325).
There is NO public way to give a v2 `Ogre::Mesh` its LOD level values:

  * `Mesh::mLodValues` is protected, with only a const getter
    (`_getLodValueArray()`, OgreMesh2.h:417);
  * `Mesh::_setLodInfo( unsigned short )` exists but its ENTIRE BODY is
    commented out (OgreMesh2.cpp:360-373) — calling it does nothing at all,
    silently;
  * the only code that writes the array is `MeshSerializerImpl` (a friend) and
    `Mesh::importV1`, i.e. loading a .mesh file off disk or converting a v1
    mesh.

Everything else is already public and already works: a `SubMesh` takes as many
VAOs as you push into `mVao[VpNormal]`/`mVao[VpShadow]`,
`SceneManager::updateAllLods` computes a LOD value per object every frame, and
`LodStrategy::lodSet` binary-searches the mesh's value array to pick the index
`RenderQueue` then uses (`vaos[meshLod]`). The ONE missing link is handing the
mesh the values to search. An application that builds its meshes in memory —
which is this application, and any importer-driven engine — can therefore build
a perfect LOD chain that Ogre will never select from.

The alternatives were weighed and rejected:
  * reaching into `mLodValues` from a derived class is impossible (`Mesh` is
    `final`) and from a friend is a fork of the pin by another name;
  * building a `v1::Mesh` and calling `importV1` re-introduces the v1 path this
    codebase deleted, runs LOD GENERATION at load time instead of using the
    levels our importer already baked, and picks a different simplifier;
  * writing a .mesh file just to read it back pays a full GPU readback per mesh
    (`BufferPacked::readRequest` has no shadow-copy short-circuit).

This restores a capability upstream commented out rather than adding a new one,
and it is a good upstream PR as it stands.

WHAT IT DOES. Adds `void Mesh::_setLodValues( const LodValueArray & )` — an
assignment, plus an OGRE_ASSERT_LOW that the array is not empty — with the
contract documented in the header: one entry per level in the SubMeshes' VAO
order, entry 0 the strategy's base value, sorted the way the active strategy
sorts, and set BEFORE any Item is created from the mesh (`Item::_initialise`
caches the array's address).

VERIFICATION. `git apply --check` against the pristine pin and `git apply
--reverse --check` after applying; `build-ogre.sh` re-run in the lane worktree;
`--engine-selftest` byte-identical (`b55e2d5d…`) — the default scene's meshes
have no LOD chain, so nothing calls it there. The behavioural gate is ATOM's own
`atom.lod_levels` suite (one mesh, the LOD bias swept, a PNG and a triangle
count per level) plus `test_engine` and the mesh suites.
diff --git a/OgreMain/include/OgreMesh2.h b/OgreMain/include/OgreMesh2.h
index e51f087aa..d2674fa88 100644
--- a/OgreMain/include/OgreMesh2.h
+++ b/OgreMain/include/OgreMesh2.h
@@ -354,6 +354,35 @@ namespace Ogre
 
         /** Internal methods for loading LOD, do not use. */
         void _setLodInfo( unsigned short numLevels );
+
+        /** Sets the LOD values this mesh's levels switch at.
+
+            The array is what LodStrategy::lodSet() binary-searches every frame
+            (through MovableObject::mLodMesh, which Item::_initialise points at
+            this array), so:
+              - it must hold one entry per LOD level, in the same order as each
+                SubMesh's mVao[VpNormal]/mVao[VpShadow];
+              - entry 0 must be the strategy's base value (see
+                LodStrategy::getBaseValue), and the rest must be sorted the way
+                that strategy sorts - ascending for the distance strategies,
+                descending for the pixel-count ones;
+              - it must be set BEFORE any Item is created from this mesh: an
+                Item caches the address of this array, and while the address
+                survives a resize through FastArray, an Item created earlier
+                would have cached an array of a different LENGTH than its
+                SubItems' VAO lists.
+
+            Without this there is no public way to give a v2 Mesh built in
+            memory its LOD levels at all: mLodValues is protected with only a
+            const getter, _setLodInfo's body is commented out, and the only
+            writers left are the serializer (a friend) and importV1(). A mesh
+            built by hand - which is every mesh in an application that does its
+            own import - could therefore carry as many VAOs per SubMesh as it
+            liked and Ogre would never select any of them.
+        @param lodValues
+            The LOD values, level 0 first.
+        */
+        void _setLodValues( const LodValueArray &lodValues );
         /** Internal methods for loading LOD, do not use. */
         // void _setSubMeshLodFaceList(unsigned short subIdx, unsigned short level, IndexData* facedata);
 
